## Why it exists

An API is a contract with programs you cannot redeploy: mobile apps already in users' pockets, partners' integrations, your own frontend in someone's cached tab. A bad function name in an internal library costs an afternoon to rename. A bad field name in a public API costs years, because removing it breaks someone.

**Mental model:** an API is a user interface for programmers. The same principles apply: consistent nouns, predictable errors, safe retries, and no surprises. Design it from the client's point of view first (write the example requests and responses before the code), and design for *change*, because you will be wrong about something.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

**Resources and methods.** Model *things* (nouns) at stable URLs and use HTTP methods for the verb: `GET /orders/42`, `POST /orders`, `PATCH /orders/42`, `DELETE /orders/42`. Method semantics come from HTTP itself (RFC 9110): `GET` is safe and idempotent (cacheable, retryable), `PUT` and `DELETE` are idempotent, `POST` is neither, `PATCH` depends on how you design it. Actions that do not map cleanly (`/orders/42/cancel`) are fine as a sub-resource `POST`; do not agonize about purity.

**Status codes people can act on.** `200` OK, `201` Created (with a `Location` header), `202` Accepted (work queued), `204` No Content, `400` malformed request, `401` not authenticated, `403` authenticated but not allowed, `404` not found, `409` conflict, `412` precondition failed, `422` well-formed but semantically invalid, `429` rate limited, `5xx` your fault. Never return `200` with an error body: clients, proxies and monitoring all key off the status code.

**One error format.** RFC 9457 *Problem Details* (`application/problem+json`) defines `type` (a stable URI identifying the problem class), `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance`, plus extension members of your choice (field-level `errors`, a `trace_id`). It replaced RFC 7807. Clients write one error handler instead of one per endpoint.

**Pagination.** Offset pagination (`?page=500&limit=20`) is simple but slow on deep pages (the database counts and skips rows) and unstable (inserts shift rows, so users see duplicates or gaps). **Keyset (cursor) pagination** asks for "rows after this position" using an indexed sort key plus a unique tiebreaker, and returns an opaque `next_cursor`. Always cap `limit`, and fetch `limit + 1` rows to know whether another page exists.

**Idempotency for unsafe requests.** Networks fail after the server acted but before the client heard back. If the client retries a `POST /payments`, you must not charge twice. The pattern (popularized by Stripe, with an IETF `Idempotency-Key` header draft) is: the client sends a unique key per logical operation; the server stores `(key, request fingerprint, response)`; a repeat with the same key and body replays the stored response; the same key with a *different* body is an error; a repeat while the first is still running returns `409`.

**Optimistic concurrency.** Two editors load the same record and both save. `ETag` on read plus `If-Match` on write lets the server reject the second write with `412 Precondition Failed` instead of silently overwriting the first (the lost update problem).

**Rate limiting.** Return `429` with `Retry-After`. The `X-RateLimit-*` headers are a convention, and the unified `RateLimit` and `RateLimit-Policy` headers are an IETF draft (not yet a final standard as far as I could verify). Clients should retry with exponential backoff and jitter, and only retry operations that are safe or idempotent.

**Evolution and versioning.** Adding an optional field, a new endpoint or a new enum value *the client is told to tolerate* is non-breaking. Removing or renaming a field, changing a type or tightening validation is breaking. Pick one versioning scheme and stick to it: a path prefix (`/v1/`), a header (GitHub uses `X-GitHub-Api-Version`), or date-based versions pinned per account (Stripe). Announce retirement with the `Sunset` header (RFC 8594) and with real notice.

**Contracts as code.** Write an **OpenAPI** document (3.1 is the common baseline; 3.2 was published in September 2025 per secondary sources I did not check against the spec site) and generate from it: validators, typed clients (`openapi-typescript`), SDKs, mock servers and reference docs. Or go code-first (FastAPI and Zod-based tools emit OpenAPI from your schemas). Either way, there is one source of truth.

**Webhooks (you calling them).** Sign each delivery (HMAC of the raw body plus a timestamp), include a unique event id, retry with backoff, and tell consumers to process idempotently by event id.

**Choosing a style.** REST over JSON is the default for public APIs. GraphQL lets clients ask for exactly the fields they need (good for many varied frontends, harder to cache and rate-limit). gRPC uses Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2 for fast, strongly typed service-to-service calls. tRPC shares TypeScript types between a TypeScript server and client with no schema file. Server-Sent Events stream one-way updates (LLM tokens) over plain HTTP.

## Worked example

A payment-creation endpoint in Express 5 with TypeScript: validated body, Problem Details errors, and idempotency. The in-memory map is for illustration; in production use the table below, because memory is lost on restart and not shared between instances.

```sql
CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (
  user_id       uuid        NOT NULL,
  key           text        NOT NULL,
  request_hash  text        NOT NULL,
  status_code   int,                       -- null while the first request is still running
  response_body jsonb,
  created_at    timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  PRIMARY KEY (user_id, key)               -- uniqueness is what makes the claim race-free
);
-- Claim the key first: INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING. Zero rows inserted means a repeat.
```

```ts
import express, { type Response } from "express";
import { createHash, randomUUID } from "node:crypto";
import { z } from "zod";

const app = express();
app.use(express.json({ limit: "100kb" }));

type Problem = { type: string; title: string; status: number; detail?: string; [k: string]: unknown };
const BASE = "https://api.example.com/problems";

function sendProblem(res: Response, p: Problem) {
  return res.status(p.status).type("application/problem+json").json(p);
}

const CreatePayment = z.object({
  amountCents: z.number().int().positive().max(1_000_000),
  currency: z.enum(["USD", "EUR", "GBP"]),
}).strict();                                     // reject unknown fields

type Stored = { hash: string; status: number; body: unknown };
const seen = new Map<string, Stored>();          // production: the idempotency_keys table

app.post("/v1/payments", (req, res) => {
  const key = req.header("Idempotency-Key");
  if (!key || key.length > 255) {
    return sendProblem(res, { type: `${BASE}/missing-idempotency-key`, title: "Idempotency-Key header required", status: 400 });
  }

  const parsed = CreatePayment.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!parsed.success) {
    return sendProblem(res, {
      type: `${BASE}/validation-error`, title: "Invalid request body", status: 422,
      errors: parsed.error.issues.map((i) => ({ path: i.path.join("."), message: i.message })),
    });
  }

  const hash = createHash("sha256").update(JSON.stringify(parsed.data)).digest("hex");
  const prior = seen.get(key);
  if (prior) {
    if (prior.hash !== hash) {
      return sendProblem(res, { type: `${BASE}/idempotency-key-reuse`, title: "Idempotency-Key reused with a different request", status: 422 });
    }
    return res.status(prior.status).set("Idempotent-Replayed", "true").json(prior.body);  // replay, do not re-charge
  }

  const payment = { id: randomUUID(), ...parsed.data, status: "pending" as const };
  seen.set(key, { hash, status: 201, body: payment });
  return res.status(201).location(`/v1/payments/${payment.id}`).json(payment);
});
```

Keyset pagination for `GET /v1/orders?limit=50&cursor=...`. The cursor is the base64url-encoded `(created_at, id)` of the last row returned; the composite index makes each page an index range scan regardless of depth:

```sql
CREATE INDEX orders_user_created_idx ON orders (user_id, created_at DESC, id DESC);

SELECT id, created_at, title
FROM orders
WHERE user_id = $1
  AND (created_at, id) < ($2, $3)        -- omit this line for the first page
ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
LIMIT 51;                                -- limit + 1: the extra row only signals "there is a next page"
```

Optimistic concurrency with `ETag`:

```http
GET /v1/orders/42            ->  200, ETag: "v7"
PATCH /v1/orders/42          If-Match: "v7"   {"status": "shipped"}
   -> 200, ETag: "v8"                        (nobody changed it meanwhile)
   -> 412 Precondition Failed                (someone else saved first; reload and retry)
```

A client that retries politely (backoff with full jitter, honoring `Retry-After`):

```ts
async function fetchWithRetry(url: string, init: RequestInit, max = 4): Promise<Response> {
  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
    const res = await fetch(url, init);
    const retryable = res.status === 429 || res.status >= 500;
    if (!retryable || attempt >= max) return res;
    const retryAfter = Number(res.headers.get("Retry-After"));
    const delayMs = Number.isFinite(retryAfter) && retryAfter > 0
      ? retryAfter * 1000
      : Math.random() * Math.min(10_000, 500 * 2 ** attempt);   // full jitter
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, delayMs));
  }
}
// Only safe for GET, or for POSTs that carry an Idempotency-Key.
```

## In real products

- **Stripe** popularized idempotency keys, expandable objects and date-pinned API versions; its API reference is the most imitated design in the industry.
- **GitHub** offers both REST (with `Link` headers for pagination and a version header) and GraphQL; **Shopify** leans on GraphQL; **Twilio** and **OpenAI-style** APIs are plain REST with API keys, `429` with retry hints, and server-sent events for streaming.
- **Frontend contracts:** a typed client generated from OpenAPI, or tRPC, so a renamed field fails the build. A **BFF** (backend for frontend) shapes data per client when one generic API would be awkward.
- **Gateways and edge:** an API gateway (AWS API Gateway, Azure API Management, Kong) centralizes auth, rate limits, request logging and versions in front of services.
- **Inside the platform:** gRPC between services, REST or GraphQL at the edge, and webhooks plus an events API for integrations.

| Style | Strength | Weak spot | Pick when |
|---|---|---|---|
| REST + JSON | universal tooling, HTTP caching, easy to debug | over/under-fetching, many round trips | public APIs, most CRUD products |
| GraphQL | clients choose fields; one round trip | caching, cost control, N+1 resolvers | many different frontends on one graph |
| gRPC | fast, typed, streaming | browsers need a proxy; opaque on the wire | internal service-to-service |
| tRPC | end-to-end TypeScript types with no schema file | TypeScript on both sides only | full-stack TypeScript apps |
| Webhooks / SSE | push instead of poll | delivery guarantees, ordering, retries | events and streaming updates |

| Pagination | Deep page cost | Stable under inserts | Jump to page N |
|---|---|---|---|
| Offset / limit | grows with the offset | no (duplicates, gaps) | yes |
| Keyset (cursor) | constant with the right index | yes | no |

> [!pitfall] Returning `200 OK` with `{"error": ...}` hides failures from clients, caches, retries and dashboards. Use the right status code and a Problem Details body.

> [!pitfall] Offset pagination on a large or fast-changing table gets slower with every page and shows duplicates when rows are inserted. Use keyset pagination for feeds and exports.

> [!pitfall] Renaming a field, tightening validation or changing a type is a breaking change even if "the API still returns 200". Add the new field, keep the old one through a deprecation window, then remove it in a new version.

> [!pitfall] In-memory idempotency checks race: two simultaneous requests with the same key can both miss and both execute. Claim the key atomically in the database (unique constraint plus `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`) before doing the work.

> [!security] Broken object-level authorization (BOLA, also called IDOR) is the top API risk: `GET /orders/42` must check that order 42 belongs to the caller, on every object endpoint, not just that the caller is logged in. Use unguessable ids as defense in depth, never as the control.

> [!security] Keep error bodies free of stack traces, SQL and internal hostnames. Log them with a `trace_id` and return only the id to the client so support can find the log.

> [!prod] Put a request id in every response (header and Problem Details `trace_id`) and in every log line. It turns "something failed around 3pm" into a one-query investigation, and it is the thread that connects your API logs to Sentry events and traces.

> [!cost] Cap `limit`, bound filter and sort options to indexed columns, and rate-limit per key. An unbounded `?limit=1000000&sort=anything` is a self-inflicted denial of service and a surprise database bill.

> [!tip] Write the OpenAPI file, or at least five example request/response pairs, before the first line of handler code. If an example looks awkward to read, it will be awkward to use.

api-grpc-graphql.md:
## Why it exists
REST over JSON is the default for public APIs, and for good reason: it is simple, cacheable and understood everywhere. But two pressures push teams toward alternatives. **Internal service-to-service calls** want compact binary messages, strict contracts, generated clients and streaming. **Client-driven products** (mobile apps, rich front ends) want to fetch exactly the fields a screen needs in one round trip instead of calling five endpoints and discarding most of each response.

**gRPC** answers the first; **GraphQL** answers the second. Neither replaces REST everywhere, and picking one should be a decision, not a fashion.

{{dg:0}}

## gRPC in one page
gRPC is a remote-procedure-call framework. You define services and messages in a **Protocol Buffers** (`.proto`) file, generate client and server code for many languages, and call remote methods like local functions. It runs over **HTTP/2** with binary protobuf messages.

```proto
syntax = "proto3";

package shop.orders.v1;

import "google/protobuf/timestamp.proto";

service OrderService {
  rpc GetOrder(GetOrderRequest) returns (Order);                       // unary
  rpc ListOrders(ListOrdersRequest) returns (stream Order);            // server streaming
  rpc UploadEvents(stream OrderEvent) returns (UploadSummary);         // client streaming
  rpc Chat(stream ChatMessage) returns (stream ChatMessage);           // bidirectional
}

message GetOrderRequest { string order_id = 1; }

message ListOrdersRequest {
  string customer_id = 1;
  int32  page_size   = 2;
}

message Order {
  string id = 1;
  string customer_id = 2;
  repeated LineItem items = 3;
  int64 total_cents = 4;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp created_at = 5;
  reserved 6, 7;            // removed fields: never reuse these numbers
  reserved "legacy_status";
}

message LineItem { string sku = 1; int32 quantity = 2; int64 unit_cents = 3; }
message OrderEvent { string order_id = 1; string kind = 2; }
message UploadSummary { int32 accepted = 1; }
message ChatMessage { string text = 1; }
```

### How the wire format shapes your rules
Protobuf identifies fields by **number**, not name. That gives compact messages and safe evolution if you follow the rules:
- **Never change or reuse a field number.** Mark removed numbers and names `reserved`.
- Adding a field is backward and forward compatible; old readers ignore unknown fields.
- Do not change a field's type incompatibly (some changes, like `int32` to `int64`, are wire compatible; many are not).
- Use `package` versions (`v1`) so a breaking redesign becomes `v2`, served alongside.

### Calling it (Go client with a deadline)
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 800*time.Millisecond)
defer cancel()

conn, err := grpc.NewClient("orders.internal:443", grpc.WithTransportCredentials(credentials.NewTLS(nil)))
if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
// NewClient (grpc-go v1.63+) replaces the deprecated Dial: it does not connect yet, the first RPC does,
// it defaults to the "dns" resolver, and WithBlock/WithTimeout are ignored. Bound each call with a deadline instead.
defer conn.Close()

client := ordersv1.NewOrderServiceClient(conn)
order, err := client.GetOrder(ctx, &ordersv1.GetOrderRequest{OrderId: "ord_123"})
if status.Code(err) == codes.DeadlineExceeded { /* the call took too long */ }
```
**Deadlines** are first-class: the remaining time propagates across downstream calls, so a slow chain stops instead of piling up work. Always set one.

### Operational notes
| Topic | What to know |
|---|---|
| **Status codes** | gRPC has its own: `OK`, `NOT_FOUND`, `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `UNAUTHENTICATED`, `PERMISSION_DENIED`, `DEADLINE_EXCEEDED`, `UNAVAILABLE` and more. `UNAVAILABLE` is the retryable one |
| **Load balancing** | HTTP/2 keeps one long connection and multiplexes calls. An L4 load balancer pins all of a client's calls to one backend. Use an L7 gRPC-aware proxy or client-side balancing |
| **Browsers** | Browsers cannot speak native gRPC. Use gRPC-Web with a proxy, or the **Connect** protocol, which also supports plain HTTP and JSON |
| **Debugging** | Binary payloads need tools: `grpcurl`, `buf curl`, server reflection, and good logging of metadata and status |
| **Health and discovery** | A standard health-checking protocol exists for load balancers and Kubernetes probes |
| **Contract tooling** | `buf` lints schemas and detects breaking changes in CI |
| **Interceptors** | Middleware for auth, logging, metrics, tracing and retries |

## GraphQL in one page
GraphQL is a query language plus a typed schema. There is usually **one endpoint**, and the client sends a query describing exactly the fields it wants.

```graphql
type Query {
  order(id: ID!): Order
  me: Customer!
}

type Customer {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  orders(first: Int = 10, after: String): OrderConnection!
}

type Order {
  id: ID!
  total: Money!
  items: [LineItem!]!
  customer: Customer!
}

type LineItem { sku: String!  quantity: Int!  product: Product! }
type Product { id: ID!  title: String!  price: Money! }
type Money { amount: Int!  currency: String! }

type OrderConnection { edges: [OrderEdge!]!  pageInfo: PageInfo! }
type OrderEdge { cursor: String!  node: Order! }
type PageInfo { endCursor: String  hasNextPage: Boolean! }

type Mutation {
  cancelOrder(id: ID!): Order!
}
```
```graphql
query OrderScreen($id: ID!) {
  order(id: $id) {
    id
    total { amount currency }
    items { quantity product { title } }
  }
}
```
One request returns exactly that shape: no over-fetching of unused fields and no second call for the products. The schema is also the documentation, and the type system powers editors and generated client types.

### The N+1 problem and DataLoader
A resolver runs per field. Resolving `items` for 50 orders, then `product` for each line item, naively fires one database query per item. The fix is **batching**: collect the keys requested in the same tick and load them together. The pattern is called **DataLoader**:

```python
import asyncio


class Loader:
    """Batch many .load(key) calls made in the same event-loop tick into one batch_fn(keys) call."""

    def __init__(self, batch_fn):
        self.batch_fn = batch_fn
        self.pending: dict = {}
        self.scheduled = False

    def load(self, key):
        loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()
        if key not in self.pending:
            self.pending[key] = loop.create_future()
        if not self.scheduled:
            self.scheduled = True
            loop.call_soon(lambda: asyncio.ensure_future(self._dispatch()))
        return self.pending[key]

    async def _dispatch(self):
        batch, self.pending, self.scheduled = self.pending, {}, False
        keys = list(batch)
        values = await self.batch_fn(keys)         # one query: SELECT ... WHERE id = ANY(keys)
        for k, v in zip(keys, values):
            batch[k].set_result(v)
```
Real libraries (DataLoader in JavaScript, Strawberry and Ariadne loaders in Python, dataloaders in Go and Java servers) add per-request caching and error handling. Create loaders **per request**, never globally, or one user's cached data can leak to another.

### Things GraphQL makes harder
| Concern | Why | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| **HTTP caching** | Usually `POST` to one URL | Persisted queries sent by `GET` with a hash, response caching by field, CDN rules |
| **Cost control** | A client can ask for deeply nested, huge queries | Depth and complexity limits, pagination caps, persisted-query allowlists, timeouts |
| **Errors** | Often HTTP 200 with an `errors` array and partial `data` | Monitor error rates in the body, not only status codes |
| **Authorization** | Every field is reachable through many paths | Enforce in the domain layer or per field, not only at the top |
| **Rate limiting** | One request can equal thousands of operations | Limit by computed cost, not request count; beware alias and batching abuse |
| **Evolution** | No versions; clients ask for fields | Add fields freely, deprecate with `@deprecated`, track field usage before removing |
| **File upload, subscriptions** | Not core to the spec | Separate endpoint or agreed extensions |

**Federation** lets many teams own parts of one graph behind a gateway (Apollo Federation and similar). It scales organizationally and adds an operational layer to run.

## REST, gRPC and GraphQL side by side
| | **REST/JSON** | **gRPC** | **GraphQL** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Best at** | Public, cacheable, resource-oriented APIs | Internal service calls, streaming, polyglot teams | Client-shaped reads across many resources |
| **Contract** | OpenAPI (optional) | `.proto` (required, generated code) | Schema (required) |
| **Wire format** | JSON text | Binary protobuf | JSON text |
| **Transport** | HTTP/1.1 or 2 | HTTP/2 | HTTP, usually POST |
| **Streaming** | SSE, WebSockets (add-ons) | Built in (four modes) | Subscriptions (extension) |
| **Browser support** | Native | Needs gRPC-Web or Connect | Native |
| **HTTP caching** | Excellent | Poor | Hard |
| **Learning curve** | Low | Medium | Medium to high (server side) |
| **Typical failure mode** | Chatty clients, many round trips | Pinned connections, schema drift without tooling | N+1 queries, expensive queries |

## Choosing
| Situation | Lean toward |
|---|---|
| Public third-party API | REST with OpenAPI, plus webhooks |
| Internal microservices, latency-sensitive, many languages | gRPC (or Connect) |
| One mobile or web client with changing screens and many backends | GraphQL (often as a backend-for-frontend) |
| Streaming data between services | gRPC streaming, or a broker for durable streams |
| Simple CRUD with a small team | REST; do not add a second paradigm without a reason |
| TypeScript monorepo, one client and one server | End-to-end typed RPC (such as tRPC) can be simpler than any of the three |

Many systems mix them: gRPC between services, a GraphQL or REST edge for clients. The rule is to keep each interface boundary's choice **explicit and justified**.

> [!pitfall] Reusing or renumbering a protobuf field. Old and new binaries then silently misread data. Reserve removed numbers and run breaking-change detection in CI.
> [!pitfall] gRPC calls with no deadline. A slow dependency then ties up threads and connections across the whole call chain.
> [!pitfall] An L4 load balancer in front of gRPC. One connection means one backend gets the traffic. Use L7 or client-side balancing and watch per-backend load.
> [!pitfall] A global DataLoader. Its cache outlives the request and can show one user another user's data. Build loaders in per-request context.
> [!pitfall] An unbounded GraphQL schema exposed publicly. Deeply nested queries or alias flooding can bring the server down. Add depth, cost and timeout limits.
> [!pitfall] Treating HTTP 200 as success in GraphQL. Read the `errors` array and alert on it.
> [!security] Disable or restrict GraphQL introspection and field-suggestion messages on public production endpoints where schema secrecy matters, and authorize at the field or domain level rather than trusting the query shape.
> [!security] gRPC internal does not mean trusted. Use TLS or mTLS between services and propagate caller identity in metadata, with authorization checks in each service.
> [!tip] Check the schema into Git and diff it in CI: `buf breaking` for protobuf, and a schema-diff tool for GraphQL, so breaking changes are caught in the pull request.
> [!cost] GraphQL shifts cost from network to server CPU and database load; one innocent query can fan out into thousands of resolver calls. Measure cost per operation, not per request.
> [!prod] Version your APIs deliberately: `v1` in the protobuf package, additive-only GraphQL changes with deprecation windows, and a documented sunset policy for both.
